    <summary>Back up a Supabase project to your own S3, R2 or B2 bucket, then restore-drill it to check the backup restores.</summary>
    <description><![CDATA[backupdrill is an open-source (MIT) command-line tool that backs up a Supabase project to a bucket you own: Amazon S3, Cloudflare R2 or Backblaze B2.

- `backupdrill backup` dumps the Postgres database and, once you add the project's Storage S3 keys, copies your Storage files. Each backup writes a checksummed `manifest.json`.
- `backupdrill drill` restores the latest snapshot into a throwaway Postgres in Docker and compares the row count of every table with the backup.
- `backupdrill restore` restores a snapshot into a Supabase project.

Data goes from your machine straight to your bucket.

This package installs the npm package `backupdrill` globally with npm, so it depends on Node.js.

## Also needed

- `pg_dump` (and `pg_restore` for drills and restores) whose major version is at least your Supabase Postgres version (Supabase runs PostgreSQL 15 to 17). Put the PostgreSQL bin folder on `PATH`, or set `BACKUPDRILL_PG_DUMP` and `BACKUPDRILL_PG_RESTORE` to the executables.
- Docker, for `backupdrill drill`.

Setup, environment variables and examples: https://github.com/backupdrill/cli#readme
]]></description>
    <releaseNotes>https://github.com/backupdrill/cli/releases</releaseNotes>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency id="nodejs-lts" version="20.0.0" />
    </dependencies>
  </metadata>
